WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:05.715
Unpredictable events can drive massive changes
in network needs and traffic patterns, especially

00:00:05.715 --> 00:00:08.010
for Communication Service Providers.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.775
To handle extreme levels of network volatility,

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:15.288
they're embracing cloud and virtualization
to deliver the 5G and edge services

00:00:15.288 --> 00:00:16.889
that their customers' demand.

00:00:17.025 --> 00:00:19.387
Also, many CSPs recognize the potential of

00:00:19.387 --> 00:00:22.867
AI to automate problem detection and remediation.

00:00:23.002 --> 00:00:26.537
Enter IBM Cloud Pak for Network Automation.

00:00:26.537 --> 00:00:30.318
Based on years of IBM experience working with
telecom partners,

00:00:30.318 --> 00:00:34.210
IBM Cloud Pak for Network Automation helps CSPs
transform their networks,

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:37.570
evolve to zero-touch operations,
reduce operating expense,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.396
and deliver services faster.

00:00:39.396 --> 00:00:41.505
What does this mean for CTOs?

00:00:41.505 --> 00:00:44.510
Their teams can rapidly design, deploy, and scale new

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.398
services in minutes instead of days, improving
customer responsiveness.

00:00:48.398 --> 00:00:51.919
But this transformation demands team players.

00:00:51.919 --> 00:00:54.112
Imagine you're a Network Service Engineer.

00:00:54.112 --> 00:00:56.380
You’re using the intent-based orchestration

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:00.511
within IBM Cloud Pak for Network Automation
to reduce the work required to launch a new

00:01:00.511 --> 00:01:02.760
network service offering.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:07.930
Bringing a new service online no longer means
racking hardware or manual installation steps.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:11.490
You start by creating a service package from
a template.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.950
It's composed of a set of
network functions,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.240
services running in containers,
or services from your Virtual Network Functions.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.590
You package and test the newly-assembled components
before deploying them to production.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:27.882
Once your team is satisfied the offering meets
customer requirements and your quality assurance

00:01:27.882 --> 00:01:31.200
standards, the fully tested package is added
to the product catalog.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.410
To save time and avoid human error, catalog
additions are done following an automated

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.495
CI/CD process.

00:01:38.495 --> 00:01:42.514
With the services available in the catalog,
your customers can order them as needed.

00:01:42.604 --> 00:01:47.630
For example, a Mobile Virtual Network Operator
could quickly order a virtual radio access

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:49.200
network, or vRAN, service.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:55.635
Or an enterprise could order a 5G Network
Slice service for a specific time AND purpose.

00:01:55.635 --> 00:01:59.340
Your customers don't need to know what's going
on behind the scenes.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.700
When the order is placed, IBM Cloud Pak for
Network Automation will decide which specific

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.740
network functions are required to deliver
that service orchestrated from scratch.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.400
Or, if the service is already up and running,
making the appropriate configuration changes.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.925
In this example, the service order and delivery
were entirely self-service.

00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:18.744
No call-in was required!

00:02:18.744 --> 00:02:22.579
Once the service is deployed, IBM Cloud Pak for Network Automation,

00:02:22.579 --> 00:02:25.151
combined with IBM Cloud Pak for Watson AIOps,

00:02:25.151 --> 00:02:27.999
automatically monitors the environment by tracking events,

00:02:27.999 --> 00:02:31.563
key performance indicators, and 
changes in the network topology.

00:02:31.834 --> 00:02:36.920
Here's the main dashboard for network operations
that interests your Site Reliability Engineers,

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.290
or SREs - it shows a real-time health status
of the network and services along with correlated

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.314
events and a service topology overview.

00:02:45.518 --> 00:02:48.968
Although automation may take place without
human intervention,

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.395
the SRE can validate what the system has done.

00:02:51.553 --> 00:02:53.969
They don't have to watch the dashboard minute-by-minute,

00:02:53.969 --> 00:02:57.760
and they have a full history of key system events.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.829
By proactively dealing with network anomalies
ahead of customer impact, your operations

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:05.260
team will have fewer fire drills and less
need for human intervention.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.000
But sometimes applying an automated resolution
isn't prudent.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:13.730
For such cases, IBM Cloud Pak for Network
Automation notifies the appropriate engineer

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:17.590
with AI-based recommendations for next best actions.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:24.230
So, to wrap this up, IBM Cloud Pak for Network
Automation can help you rapidly design, deploy,

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:26.928
and scale new services in minutes instead of days.

00:03:26.928 --> 00:03:31.205
And it helps you lower costs by moving your
team towards zero-touch operations.

00:03:31.205 --> 00:03:37.526
The best part: since it’s built on Red Hat
OpenShift, you can run it on any cloud, anywhere.

00:03:37.526 --> 00:03:41.905
Thanks for watching. If you have any questions,
please add a comment below.

00:03:41.905 --> 00:03:44.467
If you want to see more videos like this in the future,

00:03:44.512 --> 00:03:46.089
please like and subscribe.

